  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nRain continues across portions of the area, mainly central \nWyoming, as low pressure continues to move through the area. It\nis now fairly close to our office just after midnight with \nmoisture wrapping back into the Wind River Basin. Some areas \nhad a lot of rain yesterday afternoon and evening, with some \nstreet flooding in Lander. Most of the convective rain is done \nnow, and is now mainly of the stratiform variety. The main area\nof rain will continue to move slowly east through the night and\ninto tomorrow with gradually drying as the low tracks to the \neast. We will still have the chance of some flooding today as \nwell, especially later in the day if there is some \ndestabilization of the atmosphere to get some thunderstorms to \nfire. However, the impacted area will be more restricted. \nPrecipitable water values remain over 150 percent of normal \nalong and east of the Bighorns. So, we will maintain the flood \nwatch here for the rest of the day. Across the western zones, \nwith the deeper moisture moving away, we will cancel the watch \nwith issuance this morning. There will be enough moisture for \nisolated showers and storms to fire back into the basins east \nof the Continental Divide and the adjacent mountains, but \ncoverage looks rather sparse (generally less than a 1 out of 3 \nchance). Showers should gradually end this evening across the \neastern zones with all areas dry by midnight. Some locations in\nJohnson and Natrona Counties could pick up 0.75 to 1.50 inches \nof additional rain today, especially where storms persist. \nHowever, not all areas will see heavy rain.\n\nOnce we get past this evening, it looks like we will enter a \nquieter pattern. Monday looks like a nice last day of \nastronomical summer for 95 percent of the area with near normal \ntemperatures and a sunny to partly cloudy sky. There may be just\nenough moisture leftover for an isolated, and this means \nisolated with a capital I, shower or thunderstorm in and near \nthe mountains east of the Divide. The chance is at most 1 out \nof 6 though, and nothing strong is expected. The only fly in the\nointment is an upper-level low moving northeastward from the \nDesert Southwest that will open into a trough and pass through \nTuesday afternoon through Wednesday morning. The GFS has trended\ntoward the European, so the chance of showers and maybe a few \nthunderstorms looks better. This system is a fast mover so no \nheavy rain or flooding is expected out of it at this time. Any \nprecipitation from this system should clear the area by sunset \non Wednesday. Following that, ridging should build across the \narea and bring dry weather for the rest of the workweek and \npossibly into the start of next weekend. And with generally \nsouthwest flow across the area, temperatures should average \nabove normal, in some cases well above normal. Some of the \nwarmer spots may see highs in the mid or even the upper 80s from\nThursday through Saturday.",
